//? Title: Derivative-Free Search: Simplexes and Covariance Adaptation
//? Subtitle: An exact reflection/expansion step, rotated bowls and finite seed scans
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A black-box objective may be inexpensive to evaluate but offer no usable
derivatives. Search must then choose informative trial points and decide when
its local geometry has stopped changing. We derive a hand-checkable Nelder–Mead
step, compare simplex and covariance-adaptation searches on known quadratics,
and retain local-basin and seed/population sensitivity on the original bumpy and
Rastrigin objectives. Best observed trials are distinguished from global guarantees.
)
\toc

> The method

Nelder–Mead stores p+one simplex vertices. It orders their values, computes the
centroid of all but the worst, and reflects the worst through that centroid.
A particularly good reflection can be expanded; an unsuccessful one can be
contracted or followed by a simplex shrink. The initial displacement on axis j is
initial_simplex_scale*max(one,abs(x0_j)), so the numerical starting point and units
shape the initial search. The native stop test combines simplex diameter and
objective spread; neither by itself proves stationarity. Simplex storage is
O(p squared), while each comparison uses objective evaluations rather than gradients.

The retained TikZ diagram needs LaTeX/TikZ, amsmath, lmodern and dvisvgm to rebuild.
Normal documentation exports use its saved local SVG.

```{bash, file="figures/opt_derivative_free_mechanism.svg"}
set -eu
mkdir -p build
latex -interaction=batchmode -halt-on-error -output-directory=build diagrams/opt_derivative_free_mechanism.tikz > build/opt_derivative_free_mechanism.compile.log 2>&1
dvisvgm --no-fonts --bbox=10 --output=build/opt_derivative_free_mechanism.svg build/opt_derivative_free_mechanism.dvi > build/opt_derivative_free_mechanism.render.log 2>&1
printf 'Rendered the optimization mechanism diagram.\n'
```
// result_begin:
// Rendered the optimization mechanism diagram.
// \image(figures/opt_derivative_free_mechanism.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Derivative-free methods adapt a trial-point geometry from observed objective values.)
\alttext(A simplex or Gaussian distribution proposes points. Objective comparisons rank them. The algorithm replaces simplex vertices or updates Gaussian mean, covariance and scale.)

CMA-ES samples x_i=m+sigma*B*D*z_i with standard-normal z and a covariance factor
B*D. Ranked elites update m, evolution paths and covariance through weighted
rank-one/rank-mu contributions; a separate path adapts sigma. In this dense
implementation the default offspring count is four+floor(three*log(p)), six in
two dimensions, and covariance eigensolves use numerical eigenvalue floors.
It is unbounded and stochastic: covariance adaptation does not prevent a small
population from collapsing into a local basin. Covariance storage is O(p squared),
with dense eigensolve work in addition to population objective calls. Normal draws
and seeded populations are reproducible in this build, not promised bit-identical
across standard-library implementations.

> Small worked example

>> One simplex iteration reaches a scalar quadratic minimum

For f=(x-three)^2, start at zero and set initial_simplex_scale=one. Initial
vertices zero and one have values nine and four. Their one-dimensional centroid
excluding the worst is one. Reflection through it proposes two, value one;
expansion proposes three, value zero, and wins. A single native iteration therefore
returns three after four objective requests. This exact example checks the geometric
update without using a derivative.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/opt_derivative_simplex.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace o = dm::optim;
  o::QuadraticObjective f({2}, {3});
  o::NelderMeadOptions  opt;
  opt.initial_simplex_scale  = 1;
  opt.max_iterations         = 1;
  const auto          run    = o::trace_optimize<o::ArbitraryFunction>(f, o::NelderMead(opt), {0});
  const auto          trials = run.trace.values();
  std::vector<double> x      = dm::seql(-1, 5, 201).values(), y;
  for (double v : x)
    y.push_back(f.evaluate({v}));
  auto plot =
      dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
          .line(x, y, "objective", {130, 130, 130})
          .points(
              trials.numeric_column("x0").values(),
              trials.numeric_column("value").values(),
              "0, 1, reflection, expansion",
              {40, 100, 190},
              4)
          .title("One Nelder-Mead reflection and expansion")
          .x_label("trial x")
          .y_label("objective")
          .x_limits(-1, 5)
          .y_limits(0, 20)
          .legend({"quadratic", "four requested values"}, {{130, 130, 130}, {40, 100, 190}});
  plot.size(850, 490).save_svg("build/opt_derivative_simplex.svg");
  std::print("{}\n\n{}\n", run.summary().to_md(7), trials.to_md(7));
}
```
